> An update on Blacky (my pet black dragon):
> I left him locked in part of a dungeon so he wouldn't kill a co-
> aligned priest (until I wanted my money back) and when I came back for
> him I found a tame giant rat named Blacky in his place.  I couldn't
> find a trap so I think he attacked a monster that threw a poly potion
> at him.

Won't happen in vanilla - monsters don't use polymorph offensively.

>  Or he got tired of living and drank one on his own?

Won't happen in vanilla - only "puny" monsters capable of using
objects will do this. Black dragons are neither puny nor item users.

If there really was no polymorph trap, the _only_ possibility is that
your dragon ate the corpse of a chameleon or doppelganger.

> Is there a rule for behavior on this?  I know imps (even tame ones)
> will purposely jump into poly traps.  Also, this would be a nice
> function for heroes wanting to use trap doors or tele traps without a
> chance of avoiding them.

There might be a command to deliberately trigger a trap, but i don't
know (#sit on it???).

You can always trigger a teleport trap by typing <ctrl>-T, and a
trapdoor by ">". The latter won't work if you're levitating without
control.

> Also I've never seen an imp "resist" a poly trap when jumping in one
> on purpose.  Basically: what's up with that?

It's being done on purpose, so the magic resistance roll isn't
applied. Just like "avoidance" rolls and magic-protection are ignored
when a player triggers a teleport trap.

